WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.940
I think by now we are all convinced that frontier large

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:09.880
language models are extremely powerful for programming.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:13.260
You can do things that you couldn't do before,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.570
in very short times, and so on.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:19.020
So it's not only a matter of reducing the time,

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:19.990
but sometimes,

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:23.360
to do certain things you would have needed, well,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.580
many programmers for a length of

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.520
time that wasn't appropriate,

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.780
compared to what was, let's say,

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.690
the function of the project.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.860
Especially in open source,

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.770
it's not as if you had a hundred people,

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:40.260
or even three people, to throw at it for a year.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:43.510
Now, if instead you can do it in a week, the,

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:44.810
how shall I put it,

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.070
the proportions of the whole field

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:48.680
have completely changed,

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:50.700
so you can do a ton of things.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.150
LLMs are extremely powerful, and okay,

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.450
we have amply noticed that.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:00.990
But I think that at the same time all programmers have noticed

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.600
that on one side the toil of programming has shrunk,

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:08.030
which in some cases is enormous, but the fun too,

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.600
the beauty of programming has shrunk.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.350
I say the toil because sometimes it has happened to me,

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.620
for example, I remember with Disque,

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.520
I spent a month unable to compile the program,

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.830
because pieces were missing and I kept writing them,

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:25.790
and I couldn't even test whether things worked,

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.370
writing a ton of things while keeping in

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.940
mind the whole state of what I was doing,

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.620
one of the most complicated implementations

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.660
I have ever written in my life.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.200
And well, these are hard moments,

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.070
just like the heisenbugs, when you don't,

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.480
you don't know why a bug shows up,

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.410
and it shows up very rarely, because otherwise it's easy.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.090
For many years I had as my tagline on X,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.630
reproducible bugs are candies.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.690
That is, reproducible bugs are candies,

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:59.050
because bit by bit you accumulate

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.110
information about the state,

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:01.820
and well,

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.820
you understand what the problem is and you make the fix.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.510
Instead the bugs that are sometimes

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.260
there and sometimes not,

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.730
in conditions you can't figure out,

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:15.510
that maybe happen only at the customer's

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.160
and you can't manage to observe them.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.870
That is, there is a great frustration in programming,

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.830
for which many now, I'll tell you the truth,

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.900
have breathed a sigh of relief.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.280
I know many who tell me, I, honestly, well,

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.740
I was frying myself, I couldn't take it anymore.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:37.470
Now the job has become more, like, bearable.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.960
At the same time, though, the fun,

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:43.590
because there are phases of programming of incredible joy

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:47.020
and fun in what you have made while you are making it,

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:49.560
being in the flow, making those changes,

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:53.020
seeing the effects, well, we have all been through it,

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:54.710
since we were, some of us,

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.740
since we were kids and wrote our first programs.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.670
This thing here has gone down the drain,

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.060
and in these months I have asked myself a lot about

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.310
what it is that went away and how to get it back.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.340
Yesterday, for example,

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.090
I was modifying this emulator that I had written,

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.480
the Spectrum one,

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.740
where I have also implemented the emulation

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.280
of the Commodore 64 with Opus 5.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.940
At a certain point I connected it to a Raspberry

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:27.370
Pi with a very large screen that I had at home,

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.680
overclocking the SPI interface for the fastest

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:33.570
communication possible with the display,

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.180
which unfortunately is not parallel.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.050
You have to know that these displays

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:41.360
usually have a serial interface,

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:42.460
so a bit slow.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.870
Instead there are the versions

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:47.110
that have a parallel interface,

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:50.110
so you can transfer eight bits at a time,

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:52.840
and so obviously they are much faster,

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.060
but you need many more pins on

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.000
the MCU that you are using,

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:59.390
so a Raspberry Pico, for example.

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:03.210
And well, basically then I was telling it, look,

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.590
let's implement partial updates,

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.690
do the CRC of each scanline,

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.270
and if it matches don't update it.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.150
Yes, but there can be collisions.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.170
What collisions?

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:18.990
It can't happen in practice, it doesn't matter.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.880
It can happen once every, I don't know,

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:24.360
years of use that at some point there

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.690
is a line that doesn't get updated,

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:28.990
and you don't notice it at thirty,

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.620
fifty frames per second,

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:33.010
the refresh rate that you manage to

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:35.190
get even with this large display,

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.490
at least thirty frames per second,

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.450
with the other devices fifty.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.730
That is, well, this part here,

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:45.400
even if you move to the level of designer, of architect,

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:47.920
whatever the fuck we want to call it,

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.920
I have always hated this name,

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.910
but at this point there is little to be done.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.540
That is, not architect in the sense of architecture,

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:57.190
that is,

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:00.320
the idea of the software architect who sometimes

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.280
then didn't really know what the fuck he was

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.810
saying and how things were implemented,

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:09.070
because it also happened to see this in practice,

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:10.270
I didn't love it.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:13.000
But this job can be done well.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.680
One can say that, I don't know,

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:18.540
Linus is the architect of the kernel.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:20.350
Fair enough.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.870
Let's say that I am more open,

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:25.310
given how things have turned out,

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:28.120
to using this terminology in computing.

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:32.090
Anyway, you are the designer, the product person,

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:34.100
the man or woman of ideas.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.400
And this thing here, I'll tell you the truth,

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:40.660
involves me a great deal, because, I don't know,

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:42.620
I made the software keyboard,

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:45.370
but I specified exactly how I wanted it.

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:48.520
And these are not things that at this moment

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.250
large language models can do autonomously,

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:54.820
and there is great room to say, I want it transparent,

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.360
there must be this interaction, first these two keys,

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.990
it pops up, it remembers where I was the last time, go,

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:03.660
if you press on the menu,

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:06.380
let's put there the possibility to switch

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.700
between one joystick and the other.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.130
A ton of things that have to do with one's

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:14.820
own sensibility for user experience,

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:18.860
user interface, the technology, for what can be done.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:22.640
Because I, then, for these displays I am talking about,

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:24.550
what fucking beasts, damn it,

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.990
for these displays I am talking about,

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:28.990
I have written several drivers,

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:31.200
I have read the datasheets several

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:32.780
times in the last years,

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:35.060
and there were those last two years

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.130
in which I didn't work on Redis,

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:39.300
when I had thrown myself into the

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:41.120
embedded world in a big way.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.540
So this thing here is beautiful

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:45.730
in the same way as programming,

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:49.150
maybe in a different way, but the flow is there.

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:51.460
What is the problem?

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:53.390
The pauses.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.180
The problem is the pauses.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:00.590
The pauses that pass between when you write to the LLM,

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:04.540
to the agent, that you want to obtain a given result.

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:05.140
Yes,

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:07.930
then of course you check for a moment that

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.520
maybe that thing works the way you had,

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:11.230
let's say,

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:13.310
designed it and the way you had

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:15.100
expressed it in the prompt.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.440
But in fact then, I don't know,

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:19.690
it has to churn for a few minutes,

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:21.670
and meanwhile what do you do?

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:25.260
I had told you in a previous video about this,

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:29.470
that the way I defend myself from these pauses is by doing

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:33.110
meanwhile another activity that does require flow,

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.590
for example writing.

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:39.160
Lately I am writing little, so this hasn't happened much.

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:41.950
Luckily I agreed to write the preface

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.040
of a book by a friend of mine,

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:46.300
so now I am writing this preface,

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:49.790
and I was using these moments to write the preface.

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:52.630
Since there are fairly long pauses and

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:55.040
you do a completely different thing,

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:57.870
the context switching doesn't weigh on me.

00:07:58.150 --> 00:07:58.740
But well,

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:02.010
the point is that as the models become much faster,

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:03.730
almost instantaneous, and,

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:07.000
but obviously then there is also the fact that they

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.150
have to replicate these effects then on the code,

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:13.240
but often, unless, let's say, one commits, like,

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.070
the madness of using Rust, I'm joking, guys,

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:18.830
a little bit of, like, of being an asshole,

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:22.360
allow me that, which takes sixty-four years to compile,

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:25.280
otherwise there is the compilation cycle both

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:27.700
of C and of the interpreted languages,

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:31.010
if one programs at a high level, but less and less,

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:32.720
it seems to me, and so on.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.660
With Go too this cycle is very fast,

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:38.340
so you see the effects very quickly,

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.260
you can iterate this way.

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:43.200
Yes, you do get into the flow.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:46.040
Even if you don't write a line of code,

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.450
you are in the flow, but the pauses kill this stuff.

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:52.090
Sometimes obviously, I don't know,

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:54.700
for example in this embedded thing it

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:57.180
has to do the refresh of the device,

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:01.020
but it's not that it takes much time actually to do it.

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:01.890
But yes,

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:05.640
when the models become faster this thing will change.

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:08.080
There is another matter.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:11.670
Since Opus 5 is, how do you say it in English,

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.210
anal retentive, that is, basically,

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:17.780
you have to tear the words out of it with pliers,

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:21.060
otherwise it tells you nothing until the end.

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:24.890
And in the session by now what I do is I tell it,

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:29.020
every time you undertake a significant step, announce it.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:33.050
Yes, I consume more tokens, I don't give a fuck.

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:36.530
I have to see, as it goes ahead, what it is doing,

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:39.380
because understanding bit by bit which steps

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:42.220
are happening also makes you enter the flow,

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:43.420
and not only that,

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:45.740
it also makes you understand whether

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:48.160
the direction taken is the wrong one,

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:51.630
and in that case you re-prompt and tell it, hey, look,

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:55.310
this here is not what I wanted, what I was interested in,

00:09:55.330 --> 00:09:56.820
what I was looking for.

00:09:57.100 --> 00:10:00.260
Do it this way, rather do it that way.

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:02.920
But also sometimes, for example,

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:05.170
in this embedded thing I give

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:07.280
it a lot of visual feedback.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:09.420
If I know what it is doing,

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:12.890
I look at what happens on the display, I say, look,

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:16.480
here I see there is flickering, et cetera, et cetera,

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:19.960
maybe you should try, it may be that, I don't know,

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.210
at so many megahertz the protocol

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:24.050
loses a bit of information,

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:27.890
even if it usually works, sometimes it goes out of sync,

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:29.350
et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:32.660
Obviously, besides the activity of writing,

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:35.290
a very interesting activity that you

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:37.500
can do in the moments of pause,

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:40.430
when you wait while the agent is running,

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:41.710
is watching porn.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.750
No, I'm joking, maybe that too if you feel like it,

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.060
but it is writing code by hand.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:52.020
I was saying that in my opinion writing code by hand,

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:54.200
even if it is no longer useful,

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.570
remains beautiful and interesting,

00:10:56.660 --> 00:11:00.320
and one can undertake the writing of meaningful code,

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:03.020
either to learn more about programming,

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:06.880
or simply to write code that is particularly beautiful,

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:07.760
interesting.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:10.460
So you can do it this way.

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:13.690
I know, I know what many of you are thinking,

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:17.290
that actually the possibility is also that of carrying

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:20.280
forward several projects in several sessions.

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:22.190
This thing can be done,

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:25.930
and at that point you break the flow, because flow is,

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:26.890
by definition,

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:30.640
doing that thing continuously within the same project.

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:34.320
If you do context switching you lose the flow,

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:37.810
but you optimize, you no longer have dead time.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:42.410
In my opinion there is also a bit of going crazy in that,

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:42.860
well.

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:43.400
So,

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:45.520
automation must also allow us

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:47.590
to catch our breath a little.

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:50.470
If then we put ourselves, on our own,

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:54.090
into a situation more stressful than the one before,

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:57.260
I don't know to what extent that can be wise.

00:11:57.550 --> 00:11:59.900
It's something to think about.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:01.280
Bye.

